---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { articleSchema } from '../../schema';

const meta = {
    title: 'Press Release to Blog Post Examples',
    headline: 'Press Release to Blog Post Examples: Before and After',
    description:
        'Worked before-and-after examples showing how a raw funding announcement, product launch, and policy release become publishable blog posts.',
    path: '/blog/press-release-to-blog-post-examples',
    date: '2026-03-04',
};

const faqs = [
    {
        question: 'Are these examples real press releases?',
        answer:
            'They are representative examples modelled on the structure of real funding, product, and policy announcements. The transformation pattern is the same for any release that carries a headline claim, a quote, and key figures.',
    },
    {
        question: 'What is the single biggest change in every before-and-after?',
        answer:
            'The order of the facts. The release leads with the dateline and issuing body; the post leads with what changed and why it matters. The underlying facts are unchanged.',
    },
];
---

<GuideLayout
    eyebrow="Examples"
    heading={meta.headline}
    metaTitle={meta.title}
    intro={meta.description}
    breadcrumb={[
        { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
        { name: 'Blog', path: '/blog' },
        { name: meta.headline, path: meta.path },
    ]}
    faqs={faqs}
    schema={[articleSchema({ headline: meta.headline, description: meta.description, path: meta.path, datePublished: meta.date })]}
>
    <p>
        These three conversions show the same pattern applied to different announcement types. In each case the facts,
        figures, and attribution are preserved exactly; the structure and the opening are rebuilt for a reader rather
        than for a news desk.
    </p>

    <h2>Example 1: A funding announcement</h2>
    <h3>Before — raw release opening</h3>
    <blockquote>
        FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE — BENGALURU, 12 March. Acme Analytics, a provider of retail demand forecasting software,
        today announced it has raised $8 million in a Series A funding round led by Northwind Capital, with
        participation from existing investors. The company intends to use the proceeds to expand its engineering team
        and accelerate product development.
    </blockquote>

    <h3>After — converted blog post opening</h3>
    <blockquote>
        Acme Analytics has raised $8 million to expand its retail demand forecasting software. The Series A round was
        led by Northwind Capital and included existing investors. The company says the funding will go towards growing
        its engineering team and accelerating product development.
    </blockquote>
    <p>
        What changed: the dateline and “FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE” header were dropped, the amount and the lead investor
        moved from the middle to the first two sentences, and the boilerplate self-description became a short clause.
        What stayed: the exact figure, the round name, the lead investor, and the stated use of funds.
    </p>

    <h2>Example 2: A product launch</h2>
    <h3>Before — raw release</h3>
    <blockquote>
        Acme Analytics Version 4.0 is now generally available. The release introduces automated anomaly detection,
        support for hourly forecasting granularity, and a revised REST API. The product is available on all paid plans
        at no additional cost. Existing customers will receive the update automatically over the next two weeks.
    </blockquote>

    <h3>After — converted blog post</h3>
    <blockquote>
        Acme Analytics now flags demand anomalies automatically. Version 4.0, available today on all paid plans at no
        extra cost, adds automated anomaly detection, hourly forecasting granularity, and a revised REST API. Existing
        customers receive the update automatically over the next two weeks.
    </blockquote>
    <p>
        What changed: the version number moved out of the lead sentence and the capability became the headline. What
        stayed: the feature list, the pricing terms, and the two-week rollout window. Nothing was embellished — the
        post does not claim the features are faster or more accurate, because the release does not.
    </p>

    <h2>Example 3: A policy release</h2>
    <h3>Before — raw release</h3>
    <blockquote>
        Ministry of New and Renewable Energy — Government of India approves National Green Hydrogen Mission with an
        outlay of Rs. 19,744 crore. Posted On: 04 JAN 2023 3:45PM by PIB Delhi. New Delhi: The Union Cabinet, chaired
        by Prime Minister Shri Narendra Modi, has approved the National Green Hydrogen Mission. The initial outlay for
        the Mission will be Rs. 19,744 crore, including Rs. 17,490 crore for the SIGHT programme, Rs. 1,466 crore for
        pilot projects, Rs. 400 crore for R&amp;D, and Rs. 388 crore towards other Mission components. The Mission
        aims to deliver an annual production of 5 MMT of Green Hydrogen by 2030, with an associated renewable energy
        capacity addition of about 125 GW.
    </blockquote>

    <h3>After — converted blog post</h3>
    <blockquote>
        India has approved a Rs. 19,744 crore National Green Hydrogen Mission, targeting 5 MMT of annual green
        hydrogen production by 2030 and roughly 125 GW of associated renewable capacity. The Union Cabinet approved
        the mission with an initial outlay of Rs. 19,744 crore, allocated across Rs. 17,490 crore for the SIGHT
        programme, Rs. 1,466 crore for pilot projects, Rs. 400 crore for research and development, and Rs. 388 crore
        for other components.
    </blockquote>
    <p>
        What changed: the ministry name and the PIB timestamp were removed from the lead and the target figures were
        promoted to the opening line. What stayed: every rupee figure, the 5 MMT target, the 2030 deadline, and the
        125 GW capacity number. The allocation breakdown survives as a single clause instead of a run-on sentence.
    </p>

    <h2>The pattern across all three</h2>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Move</th><th>Release</th><th>Post</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Opening</td><td>Dateline and issuing body</td><td>The change and its significance</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Headline number</td><td>Mid-paragraph</td><td>First sentence</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Quote</td><td>Wherever it appeared</td><td>After the claim it supports, fully attributed</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Boilerplate</td><td>Full paragraph</td><td>Removed or condensed to a clause</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Figures</td><td>In prose</td><td>Preserved verbatim, often as a list</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Run your own</h2>
    <p>
        Paste your release into <a href="/">the converter</a> to get the parsed version, then apply the pattern above
        during review. The <a href="/blog/how-to-convert-a-press-release-into-a-blog-post">step-by-step guide</a> covers
        the full workflow, and the <a href="/blog/press-release-seo-checklist">SEO checklist</a> covers what to verify
        before publishing.
    </p>
</GuideLayout>